\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 51. One important legal distinction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 I would make this very explicit in the architecture and documentation:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \'93UDS-built consent management system\'94 \uc0\u8800  automatically \'93registered Consent Manager under DPDP.\'94
\f0\b0 \
The DPDP Act defines a Consent Manager as a separate role, and the Rules provide for registration and associated obligations. ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dpdpact2023.com/chapter-2?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 DPDP Act 2023}})\
The current framework does not require every Data Fiduciary to use a registered external Consent Manager merely because it operates its own consent system. So UDS can build its own internal consent/control platform while separately evaluating whether it ever wants to become a regulated Consent Manager itself. ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lawcodehub.com/dpdp/rules/4?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 LawCodeHub}})\
That distinction should be settled with UDS's privacy counsel before product requirements are frozen.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

